# What exactly is Pareto efficiency?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "When can nobody be made better off without someone being made worse off? Why is a world where one person owns everything Pareto efficient?"
next: {"title":"Can two people trade their way to efficiency?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/24-can-two-people-trade-their-way-to.cast"}

## Fish on the quay
domain: {"x":[0,12],"y":[0,12]}
vars:
    liv:
      value: 4
      min: 0
      max: 10
    tor:
      value: 4
      min: 0
      max: 10
sources:
    - id: pareto1906
      title: Manuale di economia politica
      authors: Vilfredo Pareto
      year: 1906
      finding: 'Sets out the optimum later called Pareto efficiency: a position from which no one can be made better off without making someone worse off. Pareto trained and worked as a railway engineer before succeeding Walras at Lausanne in 1893.'

    axes ax x_label "Liv's fish" y_label "Tor's fish" ticks.x [2,4,6,8,10] ticks.y [2,4,6,8,10] hidden true
    curve frontier #b5482e expr "10 - x" x_from 0 x_to 10 hidden true
    text lab_all "all 10 used" #b5482e at.data [8.6,3.4] font_size 30 hidden true
    curve floor #8f887c expr tor x_from 0 x_to 10 style.opacity 0 hidden true
    dot corner at.intersection_of ["floor","frontier"] radius 1 style.opacity 0 hidden true
    dot alloc #3d3833 at.x 4 at.y 4 radius 10 bind {"at.x":"liv","at.y":"tor"} style.fill #3d3833 hidden true
    label lab_alloc "{liv:0} and {tor:0}" below-left attach_to alloc font_size 30 hidden true
    region wedge x_from.ref alloc x_to.ref corner between ["floor","frontier"] style.fill #f2c14e style.opacity 0.45 hidden true
    label lab_wedge "improvements" above-right #a07d1c attach_to wedge hidden true
    arrow arr1 #2f6b8f from.x 4.2 from.y 4 to.x 5.4 to.y 4 hidden true
    label lab_arr1 "+1 Liv" below #2f6b8f attach_to arr1 hidden true
    icon fish1 at.data [9.2,10.6] size 70 set twemoji or ["tropical fish"] icon_look picture of fish hidden true
    icon fish2 at.data [10.6,10.6] size 70 set twemoji or ["tropical fish"] icon_look picture of fish hidden true
    text lab_rot "2 rotting" #c96567 at.data [9.9,9.3] font_size 28 hidden true
    group quay members ["fish1","fish2","lab_rot"] hidden true
    portrait pareto x 870 y 300 width 190 of "Vilfredo Pareto" cameo true hidden true
    text lab_eff "Pareto efficient" #b5482e at.data [8.6,3.4] font_size 30 hidden true
    text not_good "efficient ≠ good" #3d3833 at.data [9.5,7] font_size 34 hidden true
    text manuale "Manual, 1906" #3d3833 cites ["pareto1906"] x 870 y 445 font_size 26 hidden true

    card title "Fish on the quay"

When can you help someone without hurting anyone? And how do you know when you've run out of such moves? Take two Harbourtown fishers, Liv and Tor, and one day's catch.
    draw ax floor corner

B: Ten fish, say. Liv ta (@draw alloc lab_alloc@)kes four, Tor takes four, and two are (@draw quay@) left to rot on the quay.

Hand one (@draw arr1 lab_arr1@) of the rotting fish to Liv. She's better off, and Tor hasn't lost a thing.

B: That's a Pareto improvement: someone gains and nobody loses. It's (@draw pareto@) named after Vilfredo Pareto, an Italian railway engineer who turned economist.

B: He set out the idea in his 190 (@draw manuale@)6 Manual of Political Economy.

    erase pareto arr1 lab_arr1 manuale

From four and fou (@draw wedge lab_wedge@)r, every split up and to the right is an improvement: more for one, and no less for the other.

B: Until the fish run  (@draw frontier lab_all@)out. Once all ten are handed out, every fish for Liv has to come from Tor.

    erase lab_wedge

    erase quay

So on (@animate liv 5 tor 5 duration 3@) that line the wedge shrinks to nothing. No move is left that helps one without hurting the other.

    erase lab_all

B: And that's Pareto effici (@draw lab_eff@)ency. Not fair, not best: just no free improvement left.

    pause 0.4

    hide quay

    quiz "Liv has six fish, Tor has two, and two lie on the quay. Which move is a Pareto improvement?"
        right: Tor takes a quay fish: he gains and Liv keeps her six. The other two both cost Liv a fish.
        wrong: Look for the move where nobody ends up with fewer fish: here, Tor takes a quay fish.
        + Tor takes a quay fish
        * Liv gives Tor one of hers
        * Split all ten, five and five

Most people hear 'efficient' and think (@draw not_good@) 'good'. The economist's word is much narrower, and the narrowness has odd consequences.

    erase not_good

Only the fish on the (@draw quay@) quay make free improvements.
    animate liv 4 tor 4 duration 1.5

    erase quay

Once t (@animate liv 6 tor 4 duration 1.5@)he quay is empty, the split is efficient, whatever it is.

    highlight frontier lab_eff

## All ten to Liv
domain: {"x":[0,12],"y":[0,12]}
vars:
    liv:
      value: 4
      min: 0
      max: 10
    tor:
      value: 4
      min: 0
      max: 10
sources:
    - id: sen1970
      title: Collective Choice and Social Welfare
      authors: Amartya Sen
      year: 1970
      finding: 'An economy can be Pareto optimal even when some are rolling in luxury and others near starvation: ''a society or an economy can be Pareto-optimal and still be perfectly disgusting'' (p. 22).'

    axes ax x_label "Liv's fish" y_label "Tor's fish" ticks.x [2,4,6,8,10] ticks.y [2,4,6,8,10] hidden true
    curve frontier #b5482e expr "10 - x" x_from 0 x_to 10 hidden true
    text lab_eff "Pareto efficient" #b5482e at.data [8.6,3.4] font_size 30 hidden true
    curve floor #8f887c expr tor x_from 0 x_to 10 style.opacity 0 hidden true
    dot corner at.intersection_of ["floor","frontier"] radius 1 style.opacity 0 hidden true
    dot ptC #b5482e at.x 10 at.y 0 radius 11 style.fill #b5482e hidden true
    text lab_C "all to Liv" #b5482e at.data [10.4,1.3] font_size 30 hidden true
    arrow arrC #2f6b8f from.x 10 from.y 0 to.x 9.1 to.y 0.9 hidden true
    text lab_arrC "Liv −1" #2f6b8f at.data [8,0.4] font_size 28 hidden true
    dot p55 #b5482e at.x 5 at.y 5 radius 8 style.fill #b5482e hidden true
    dot p010 #b5482e at.x 0 at.y 10 radius 8 style.fill #b5482e hidden true
    dot alloc #3d3833 at.x 4 at.y 4 radius 10 bind {"at.x":"liv","at.y":"tor"} style.fill #3d3833 hidden true
    label lab_alloc "{liv:0} and {tor:0}" below-left attach_to alloc font_size 30 hidden true
    arrow arrAC dashed #8f887c from.x 4.3 from.y 3.7 to.x 9.6 to.y 0.4 hidden true
    text lab_arrAC "Tor −4" #8f887c at.data [6.2,1.6] font_size 28 hidden true
    region wedge x_from.ref alloc x_to.ref corner between ["floor","frontier"] style.fill #f2c14e style.opacity 0.45 hidden true
    curve stretch #e0a800 expr "10 - x" x_from 4 x_to 6 bind.x_from liv bind.x_to "10 - tor" style.stroke_width 9 hidden true
    text sen "“can be Pareto optimal and\nstill be perfectly disgusting”\n— Amartya Sen, 1970" #3d3833 at.data [7.6,9.8] cites ["sen1970"] width 560 font_size 34 hidden true

    card title "All ten to Liv"

B: So efficient just means the quay is empty. Now take it to the end of the line: Liv take (@draw ptC lab_C@)s all ten, and Tor gets none.
    draw ax floor corner frontier lab_eff

Surely that can't count as efficient. Tor has nothing!
    highlight ptC lab_C effect circle

    quiz "Liv has all ten fish and Tor has none. Is that split Pareto efficient?" voice b
        right: Yes: every fish Tor could get would have to come from Liv.
        wrong: Is there a fish that could go to Tor without leaving Liv with fewer? There isn't: so yes, it's efficient.
        + Yes
        * No

B: It does count. To give Tor a single fis (@draw arrC lab_arrC@)h, you'd have to take it from Liv, so no move is left that hurts nobody.

So the end of the li (@highlight ptC@)ne passes the test like any other point on it.
    erase arrC lab_arrC

B: Every split on the line pass (@draw p55@)es: ten and none, five  (@draw p010@)and five, none and ten. Pare (@highlight frontier@)to's test won't choose among them.

    erase p55 p010

Then compare all-to-Liv with  (@draw alloc lab_alloc@)the wasteful four and four. Surely it's at least better than that?

B: Pareto can't say. Going from fou (@draw arrAC lab_arrAC@)r and four to all-to-Liv makes Tor worse off, so it isn't an improvement.

    erase arrAC lab_arrAC

B: The improvement (@draw wedge@)s on four and four reach only t (@draw stretch@)his short stretch of the line.

So an efficient split needn't beat an inefficient one. It just has nothing left to give away for free.
    highlight ptC alloc effect circle

B: Amartya Sen put it b (@draw sen@)luntly in 1970: a society can be Pareto optimal and still be perfectly disgusting.

    pause 0.5

    erase sen

Try it: drag the split around, and watch which part of the line its improvements can reach.
    explore params ["liv","tor"]

    animate liv 3 tor 3 duration 1.5

    quiz "Liv has three fish, Tor has three, and four rot on the quay. Which split is a Pareto improvement on that?"
        right: Three for Liv, seven for Tor: Liv keeps her three and Tor gains four. The other two cost Tor fish.
        wrong: Check each person: does anyone end up with fewer than three? Here, three for Liv and seven for Tor.
        * Six for Liv, two for Tor
        + Three for Liv, seven for Tor
        * Nine for Liv, one for Tor

So efficiency rules out waste, and nothing else. Who gets the fish is a different question.
    highlight frontier lab_eff

## Who has to lose?
domain: {"x":[0,14],"y":[0,14]}
vars:
    liv:
      value: 5
      min: 0
      max: 12
    tor:
      value: 5
      min: 0
      max: 12
    total:
      value: 10
      min: 10
      max: 12
sources:
    - id: kaldor1939
      title: Welfare Propositions of Economics and Interpersonal Comparisons of Utility
      authors: Nicholas Kaldor
      year: 1939
      finding: 'A change is an improvement if the gainers could compensate the losers and still be better off, whether or not compensation is paid (Economic Journal 49(195): 549-552).'
    - id: hicks1939
      title: The Foundations of Welfare Economics
      authors: John R. Hicks
      year: 1939
      finding: 'Proposes the principle of hypothetical compensation as a foundation for welfare economics (Economic Journal 49(196): 696-712).'
    - id: scitovsky1941
      title: A Note on Welfare Propositions in Economics
      authors: Tibor Scitovsky
      year: 1941
      finding: 'The compensation test can approve a move from A to B and also the move back from B to A (Review of Economic Studies 9(1): 77-88).'

    axes ax x_label "Liv's fish" y_label "Tor's fish" ticks.x [2,4,6,8,10,12] ticks.y [2,4,6,8,10,12] hidden true
    curve old #d0865f expr "10 - x" x_from 0 x_to 10 style.opacity 0.45 hidden true
    curve frontier #b5482e expr "total - x" x_from 0 x_to 10 bind.x_to total hidden true
    text lab_total "{total:0} fish" #b5482e at.data [12.6,1.8] font_size 30 hidden true
    dot start #8f887c at.x 5 at.y 5 radius 8 style.fill #8f887c hidden true
    text lab_start "before: 5 and 5" #8f887c at.data [2.6,4.3] font_size 28 hidden true
    curve floor5 expr "5" x_from 0 x_to 12 style.opacity 0 hidden true
    dot corner5 at.intersection_of ["floor5","frontier"] radius 1 style.opacity 0 hidden true
    region wedge x_from.ref start x_to.ref corner5 between ["floor5","frontier"] style.fill #f2c14e style.opacity 0.45 hidden true
    dot alloc #3d3833 at.x 5 at.y 5 radius 10 bind {"at.x":"liv","at.y":"tor"} style.fill #3d3833 hidden true
    label lab_alloc "{liv:0} and {tor:0}" below-left attach_to alloc font_size 30 hidden true
    text lab_minus "Tor −1" #c96567 at.data [6.6,2.5] font_size 30 hidden true
    text kh "Kaldor, Hicks 1939:\ncould winners pay losers?" #2f6b8f at.data [10.2,11.6] cites ["kaldor1939","hicks1939"] font_size 30 hidden true
    arrow pay #2f6b8f from.x 7.9 from.y 4.1 to.x 7.1 to.y 4.9 hidden true
    text lab_pay "could pay Tor 1" #2f6b8f at.data [9.6,6] font_size 30 hidden true
    text ppi "potential Pareto improvement" #2f6b8f at.data [9.4,11.6] font_size 32 hidden true
    text scit "Scitovsky 1941:\nthe test can OK a move\nand then the move back" #3d3833 at.data [9.8,9.6] cites ["scitovsky1941"] font_size 28 hidden true
    text sure1 "Only rules out waste: consensus" #3d7a3d at.data [8.6,11.6] font_size 32 hidden true
    text sure2 "Unpaid compensation counts? contested" #b5482e at.data [8.6,10.2] font_size 32 hidden true
    cards mathq options [{"text":"Show me the math","goto":"math"},{"text":"Skip it","goto":"test"}] then test hidden true
    scratch math top-right work [{"tex":"x \\text{ efficient: no feasible } y \\text{ with}"},{"tex":"U_i(y) \\ge U_i(x) \\ \\forall i, \\ U_j(y) > U_j(x) \\text{ for some } j"},{"tex":"\\text{Kaldor–Hicks: } \\textstyle\\sum_i \\Delta W_i > 0"},{"tex":"+3 - 1 = +2 > 0"}] font_size 26 hidden true

    card title "Who has to lose?"

B: Pareto's test never takes a fish from anyone. That sounds harmless, until you notice that almost every real policy has a loser.
    draw ax floor5 frontier lab_total alloc lab_alloc

Say the harbour council builds a brea (@draw old start@)kwater, and the da (@animate total 12 duration 2.5@)y's catch grows from ten fish to twelve. Illustrative numbers again.

B: Liv's (@draw lab_start@) berth gets the (@animate liv 8 tor 4 duration 3@) shelter: she goes from five fish to eight. Tor's gets worse: from five to four.

Two more fish in total, but Tor (@draw lab_minus@) is down one. So it's not a Pareto im (@draw corner5 wedge@)provement, and under Pareto's rule one loser blocks it.

B: In 1939 Nicholas Kaldor an (@draw kh@)d John Hicks proposed a looser test: could the winners compensate the losers and still come out ahead?

Here they could. Liv ha (@draw pay lab_pay@)nds Tor one fish back: seven and five, and nobody is wor (@highlight wedge@)se off than before the breakwater.

    erase kh

B: That's a potent (@draw ppi@)ial Pareto improvement, and it's the logic behind cost-benefit analysis. But could isn't did: if Liv  (@highlight alloc lab_minus effect circle@)keeps the fish, Tor is still down one.

    erase ppi lab_pay pay

And in 1941 Tibor Scit (@draw scit@)ovsky showed the looser test can approve a move, and then approve the move back again.

    erase scit

B: So how sure are we? That Pareto efficiency  (@draw sure1@)only rules out waste and says nothing about fairness: consensus.

Whether a compensation  (@draw sure2@)that's never paid should count as a gain: contested.

    pause 0.5

    erase sure1 sure2

    fade ax old frontier lab_total start lab_start wedge alloc lab_alloc lab_minus to 0.12

    draw mathq

    ask "Show the math, or skip it?" on mathq stage own voice b
        intro: There's a compact way to write both tests.

@math

    hide mathq

A split x is Pareto e (@draw math_box math_line_1 math_line_2@)fficient if no feasible split y gives everyone at least as much, and someone more.

Kaldor and Hicks ask less: (@draw math_line_3@) add up the gains and losses, and the sum must be positive.

The breakwater: Liv plus three, Tor mi (@draw math_line_4@)nus one, a sum of plus two. It passes, though Tor loses.

    erase math

@test

    hide mathq

    quiz "A concert hall sells 80 of its 100 seats. Twenty stay empty, while fans outside would gladly sit in them. Pareto efficient?" intro "Three new cases." voice b
        right: No: an empty seat could go to a fan outside, and nobody would be worse off.
        wrong: Can someone gain with nobody losing? Here: no, not efficient.
        * Yes, every buyer got a seat
        + No, a seat could go to a fan and nobody loses
        * Can't tell without knowing incomes

    quiz "A king owns all the land, his subjects own none, and every field is farmed. Pareto efficient?"
        right: It can be: every field is used, so helping a subject means taking from the king.
        wrong: Pareto asks only: can someone gain with nobody losing? Here: it can be efficient.
        * No, it's far too unequal
        + It can be: any change takes land from the king
        * No, the subjects could be better off

    quiz "A new road saves drivers $10 million a year and costs the farmers whose land it crosses $2 million. Nobody pays the farmers. What is it?" voice b
        right: A potential Pareto improvement: the drivers could pay the farmers and still gain, but the farmers still lose.
        wrong: Does anyone lose? Could the winners pay them? Here: a potential one.
        * A Pareto improvement
        + A potential Pareto improvement
        * Neither

    fade ax old frontier lab_total start lab_start wedge alloc lab_alloc lab_minus to 1

So what exactly is Pareto efficiency? No free improvement left: nothing wasted, and nothing said about who has what. When economists say a market works, that narrow thing is what they mean first.
    highlight frontier wedge

